---
// /society — The Society: a mind of mindless parts, live in the browser.
// Prototype (2026-08-05). Launched unlisted — a new experiment shows itself before it
// claims a shelf place — and listed in the /holdings register on Frank's go the next
// morning (src/data/werke.ts, ranked third).
import Page from '@/layouts/Page.astro'
import SocietyFigure from '@/components/society/SocietyFigure.astro'
import GrowthRecord from '@/components/society/GrowthRecord.astro'
import { STAGES } from '@/lib/society/stages'
import { awakeChapters, CHAPTERS } from '@/lib/society/agents'

// The kicker is derived, never typed: it said "stage 2" while stage 3 was live (Frank
// caught it, 2026-08-06). The growth record is the single source of what stage this is.
const stages = STAGES.filter((s) => s.kind === 'stage')
const current = stages[stages.length - 1]
const started = STAGES[0].date

// The shelf's count is derived too — it read "eleven" while twelve chapters were awake
// the moment a stage woke one. The roster is the only place that knows.
const WORDS = [
  'no',
  'one',
  'two',
  'three',
  'four',
  'five',
  'six',
  'seven',
  'eight',
  'nine',
  'ten',
  'eleven',
  'twelve',
  'thirteen',
  'fourteen',
  'fifteen',
  'sixteen',
  'seventeen',
  'eighteen',
  'nineteen',
  'twenty',
]
const spell = (n: number) => WORDS[n] ?? String(n)
const awakeCount = awakeChapters().size
const asleepCount = CHAPTERS.length - awakeCount
---

<Page
  title="The Society | Frank Bültge"
  description="A mind of mindless parts, running live in your browser — after Marvin Minsky's The Society of Mind (1986), forty years on. It perceives you; you may silence any of its agents."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-14">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-fg-muted">
        experiment · stage {current.n} — {current.title.replace(/^The /, '')} · started {started}
      </p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold">The Society</h1>
      <p class="mt-1 text-fg-muted">a mind of mindless parts</p>

      <blockquote class="mt-6 border-l-2 pl-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        “What magical trick makes us intelligent? The trick is that there is no trick. The
        power of intelligence stems from our vast diversity, not from any single, perfect
        principle.”
        <footer class="mt-1 text-xs">
          — Marvin Minsky, <cite>The Society of Mind</cite>, §30.8 (1986)
        </footer>
      </blockquote>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="font-semibold">This is Minsky's book, performed.</strong> Not a
        simulation inspired by <cite>The Society of Mind</cite> (1986) but that book as
        working machinery: twenty-seven agents, every one small enough to read whole, every
        one carrying the essay it steps out of. They build block towers, quarrel over a
        single hand, and remember what worked. None of them can build. A staged reading, in
        the sense a score is read by being played.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Forty years ago Minsky claimed a mind is a society of such mindless parts. Today's
        machine minds bet on the opposite: one vast, opaque model. This runs the
        counter-model in a glass case — the whole of it readable, down to the last rule.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        You are in it. Your pointer is a shadow in the block world: the society notices
        motion without ever knowing what moves, grows curious, gets bored of you, and
        startles if you are sudden. And you may hurt it: select any agent and silence it —
        what the whole loses is not scripted anywhere; it follows from the missing rule. A
        reload heals everything.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        If you have a minute: watch until a tower stands. If you have five: silence GRASP,
        or the censor that holds the wrecker back, and watch what a mind is without it.
      </p>
      {/* The room exit (/society/room) exists and works, but is NOT advertised yet: its
          staging — tempo, typography, the camera on the tower — is still rough (Frank,
          2026-08-07). The route stays reachable so it can be worked on and projected; the
          link goes back the moment the staging earns it. */}
    </header>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <SocietyFigure />
    </div>

    <section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl" aria-label="Under proof">
      <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-fg-muted">
        under proof
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        The piece's claims are tests, not captions
        (<code class="text-xs">src/lib/society/engine.test.ts</code>): the same seed is the
        same morning, tick for tick · left alone, the society finishes a tower, though no
        agent can build · an intact society wrecks only finished work; silence
        CENSOR-WRECK and it smashes its own unfinished work · silence GRASP and no block is
        ever placed again · silence SEE-MOTION and you become invisible to it. Stage 2, the
        transfer (§8.6): after two towers the society turns to Minsky's arch — its
        practiced hands carry over to the new goal, but its first move still goes to the
        old tower site, because K-lines remember towers, not arches; what memory transfers
        is the middle, never the plan. Silence ARCHER and this society builds towers until
        the end of its days. Chapter and section numbers are verified against the 1986
        edition; the thirty-chapter shelf
        shows honestly which parts of the book live here so far — {spell(awakeCount)} chapters
        are awake, {spell(asleepCount)} are roadmap. A full reading pass over the print
        edition stands behind the citations
        (<code class="text-xs">docs/society/reading-protocol.md</code>).
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-fg-muted">
        what the attachment does not show
      </h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        Hold a sign and you become a parent — but there is no attachment-figure here, only a
        signal. Minsky's mechanism turns on the child learning to tell the parent from
        everyone else; this society has no face, no voice, no social realm in which anyone
        could be someone. It takes any sign as a parent's, so it cannot stage §17.2's actual
        experiment, where the same scolding from a stranger and from a parent teach
        different things. It feels no guilt and no shame — it only changes what it wants.
        Attachment here redistributes worth; it never grows a goal no one wrote. You cannot
        praise it for resting, because rest is an appetite and has no goal to make worthier
        — a sign held then lands on nothing, and the scribe says so. And a reload heals
        everything, which is a mercy in an artwork and a lie about attachment, whose effects
        do not wash out. Minsky's own warning in §9.1 is aimed at us: “we have to be careful
        not to accept those signs at their face value.” This society accepts them
        completely. It has no censor for its parent.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl" aria-label="Where this stands">
      <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-fg-muted">
        where this stands
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        The mechanisms here are borrowed, and gladly: agent societies are a deep canon in
        art (Ian Cheng's <cite>BOB</cite>, Karl Sims, <cite>A-Volve</cite>,
        <cite>Creatures</cite>), ablation is a standard scientific gesture (the
        <cite>Neural Interactome</cite>, 2017, lets you shift-click a neuron out of a
        simulated nervous system), and the book has been published interactively before —
        the Voyager CD-ROM of 1994, made with Minsky, navigated by its chapters. What we
        looked for and could not find is the book <em>running</em>: its own agents at work
        and ablatable, each rule bearing the essay it comes from. Prior editions display
        the book without implementing it; prior implementations take its ideas and drop the
        book. And no one, in art or in science, mourns what they switch off — here every
        silenced agent has an elegy. The searches, including everything that refuted our
        first claims, are in <code class="text-xs">docs/society/prior-art.md</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GrowthRecord />
  </article>
</Page>
